---
## 5. Application to Makemore v2: Embedding Reshapes

In Makemore v2, we have:
- Batch size $B$ (e.g. 32)
- Context block size $T$ (e.g. 3 characters)
- Embedding dimension $C$ (e.g. 2 features per character)

When we embed our inputs $X \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times T}$, we get:
$$\text{emb} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times T \times C}$$

To feed this into a linear layer $W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{(T \cdot C) \times H}$, we need to concatenate the $T$ embeddings for each example into a flat vector of length $T \cdot C$.

### Bad Approach (Memory Copying):
`torch.cat([emb[:, 0, :], emb[:, 1, :], emb[:, 2, :]], dim=1)` -> creates 3 slices, allocates new tensor memory, copies data.

### First-Principles Approach (Zero-Copy View):
`emb.view(B, T * C)` -> modifies only `shape` and `stride` metadata, executes in **0 nanoseconds**, 0 memory allocated!

# %% [code] cell:10
B, T, C = 4, 3, 2
# Simulated embedding output
emb = torch.randn(B, T, C)

print("Embedding Tensor Shape: ", emb.shape)
print("Embedding Tensor Stride:", emb.stride())

# Flattening with view
emb_flat = emb.view(B, T * C)
print("\nFlattened View Shape: ", emb_flat.shape)
print("Flattened View Stride:", emb_flat.stride())
print("Shares storage?       ", emb.untyped_storage().data_ptr() == emb_flat.untyped_storage().data_ptr())

